---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";

const title = "Meditation Safety and Gentle Practice";
const description =
  "Echo Buddha's meditation safety guidance for educational practice, grounding, adapting, stopping, and seeking qualified support.";
const canonicalPath = "/meditation-safety/";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}${canonicalPath}`,
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url
  }
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <a class="back-link" href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Meditation safety</span>
      <h1>Meditation Safety and Gentle Practice</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Echo Buddha meditation content is educational and reflective. It should support gentle practice without
        promising cure, treatment, guaranteed calm, guaranteed sleep, or any medical outcome.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Educational Practice, Not Treatment</h2>
      <p>
        Meditation can be meaningful for many people, but experiences vary. Echo Buddha does not provide diagnosis,
        therapy, crisis support, or individualized medical or mental health advice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pages about stress, sleep, anxiety, anger, grief, overthinking, or difficult emotions should be read as general
        educational guidance. They should not be used to delay or replace qualified support.
      </p>

      <h2>Stop, Ground, or Adapt</h2>
      <p>
        If breath focus, stillness, silence, loving-kindness, body awareness, or a difficult emotion feels
        overwhelming, stop or adapt the practice. Open your eyes, feel the feet or hands, notice the room, change to a
        neutral anchor, shorten the session, stand up, or return to ordinary activity.
      </p>
      <p>
        A useful meditation session is not the one that looks calm from the outside. It is the one practiced with
        honesty, patience, and care for the actual body and mind present today.
      </p>

      <h2>Trauma Sensitivity and Overwhelm</h2>
      <p>
        Some practices can stir fear, numbness, grief, intrusive memories, shame, or emotional flooding. If that
        happens, forcing the practice is not required. Choose grounding, connection, movement, or qualified support
        instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Loving-kindness or forgiveness practices should not be used to excuse harm, reopen unsafe contact, remove
        boundaries, or pressure someone into reconciliation.
      </p>

      <h2>When to Seek Support</h2>
      <p>
        Seek qualified professional support for serious, persistent, unsafe, or worsening distress. If there is
        immediate danger, use local emergency or crisis resources rather than relying on a website practice.
      </p>

      <h2>How This Applies Across Echo Buddha</h2>
      <p>
        Meditation pages, wellbeing-adjacent articles, and reflective practices should include gentle limitation
        language where relevant. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> for broader limitations and
        <a href="/corrections/">Corrections and Editorial Updates</a> to report a safety concern.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back-link {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
</style>
